          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think It Over"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.705,
            0.267,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": "Are atoms the smallest indivisible particles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.083,
            0.754,
            0.255,
            0.848
          ],
          "content": "Why do electrons not fall into the nucleus even though they are attracted to protons in it?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.85,
            0.259,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": "Why did scientists keep modifying atomic models?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.307,
            0.683,
            0.373,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "4 **Grade 8** Curiosity **Chapter 7**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.379,
            0.68,
            0.89,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "Everything that you see or **observe**, or feel around is **matter**. You have learnt that matter consists of tiny par c es ca e a oms. a e a c oser oo a e p c ure ti l ll d t T k l l k t th i t"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.722,
            0.89,
            0.801
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "given above. What do you observe? Do you **notice** that both living beings, like us, and non-living things, like a house, are ultimately composed of atoms? These atoms are so tiny that they cannot be seen with the naked eye."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.297,
            0.806,
            0.89,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "You may be wondering — Is an atom truly the smallest unit of matter, or can it be divided even further?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.296,
            0.844,
            0.89,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": "Scientists, too, have been exploring whether atoms are divisible. If so, what are their constituents and how are these arranged? Let us **examine** how the concept of atoms emerged and how it has been evolved since then."
        }
      ]
    }
